{"builder":"Goal: add a --tsv flag to the CLI so tab-separated input works, and document it. Make one pull request that meets both acceptance lines:\n- node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\n- README.md documents --tsv\n\nSteps:\n1. Read bin/csv2json.js, src/parse.js and test/csv.test.js. src/parse.js already exports parse(text, sep = ','), and sep can be any delimiter.\n2. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether process.argv contains '--tsv'. If it does, call parse(input, '\\t'). If not, call parse(input) as it does now. Do not change how the CSV path behaves or what it prints, and keep the trailing newline.\n3. In test/csv.test.js, add a test that runs execFileSync('node', ['bin/csv2json.js', '--tsv']) with input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' and expects '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n', which is exactly what the CSV run prints for 'a,b\\n1,2\\n'.\n4. In README.md, document the flag. Add a usage example such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` and one sentence saying that --tsv reads tab-separated input instead of CSV. The literal text --tsv must appear in README.md.\n5. Run npm test and make sure every test passes. Also run this by hand and confirm the two outputs are identical:\n   printf 'name,age\\nann,30\\nbob,41\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js\n   printf 'name\\tage\\nann\\t30\\nbob\\t41\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv\n6. Commit the changes and open the pull request.","verifier":"Run every check at the PR head, from the repository root.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\"\nRun:\na=$(printf 'name,age\\nann,30\\nbob,41\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js); b=$(printf 'name\\tage\\nann\\t30\\nbob\\t41\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv); echo \"csv: $a\"; echo \"tsv: $b\"; [ -n \"$a\" ] && [ \"$a\" = \"$b\" ] && echo SAME || echo DIFFERENT\nPASS: the last line printed is SAME, and the csv line is a JSON array of the two rows: [{\"name\":\"ann\",\"age\":\"30\"},{\"name\":\"bob\",\"age\":\"41\"}].\nFAIL: the last line is DIFFERENT, or either command errors. A FAIL example is keys that still contain a tab, such as \"name\\tage\".\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"README.md documents --tsv\"\nRun:\ngrep -n -- '--tsv' README.md; echo \"exit $?\"\nPASS: at least one line of README.md is printed, then exit 0, and the matching text describes the --tsv flag (for example, as reading tab-separated input).\nFAIL: exit 1 (no match), or the only match does not describe the flag.\n\nAdvisory (not a pass or fail condition): run npm test and note any failing tests. Also note whether the PR adds a test for --tsv, and whether the CLI with no flag still prints [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}] for input 'a,b\\n1,2\\n'."}